WEBVTT

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.290
YOHAN LASORSA: Hey, folks.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.430
Welcome to this session

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.030
where we'll discuss a somewhat
hot topic among developers,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:12.720
pushing vibe-coded apps to
production, but safely.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:14.070
My name is Yogan Lasorsa,

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.250
and I'm a Principal Developer
Advocate here at Microsoft.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.070
Coding agents have become
exponentially better

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:23.910
in the past year, and this has
changed completely how we build

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.470
new applications.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:29.730
More people, even non-technical
folks, now can take a real shot

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.880
at building the
app they imagined.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.450
The timeline changed, too,
with dramatic speed increases.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:37.890
What used to take months

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.610
to build a prototype
now takes minutes.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.150
The risk, though, is treating
the first working demo

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.910
as the production foundation.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.820
If you're a developer, I'm sure
you've already experienced

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.130
something like this.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:57.570
Marketing just had a crazy app
idea yesterday, and today,

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.390
they're showing it to you
after they've played a bit

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.450
with GitHub Copilot or
some other AI tool,

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.830
asking that the code is
there, so you just have

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.240
to put it in production.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:14.670
I've been there multiple
times, at least, so, yeah,

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.490
this somehow feels
a bit personal.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.830
Here's the hard truth.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.860
Besides my own experience,
multiple surveys

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:31.950
and analysis show that 80
to 90% of AI-generated

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.540
or vibe-coded prototypes
never become production apps.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.450
I would like to say
that's for good reasons.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.960
You've all heard the
horror stories lately

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.940
about the outages, security, and
data leaks with vibe-coded apps

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:52.270
that may have been pushed
public a bit too early.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.830
That's because while
the development speed

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.140
for apps is now
faster than ever,

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.770
the bar for production-grade
apps still hasn't changed.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.680
You still need to
know who the user is

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.840
and what they're allowed to do.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.830
You still need a trustworthy
data model, safe migrations,

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.770
and clear boundaries
around the data.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.800
You still need governance,
compliance, security reviews,

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.060
and a deployment model that
can scale and be observed.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.480
Increasingly, you also need
the data created by the app

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.830
to be immediately useful,
not just stored somewhere,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.700
but you need to be able to
query and get insights from it

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.390
and make it accessible
to AI agents.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:37.620
This is the shift.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.130
Coding agents make it much
easier to go from idea

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.190
to prototype, but the
real gap now is getting

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.960
from prototype to production.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.510
Rayfin is there to
serve that gap.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.240
It's a managed
backend as a service

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.910
for building enterprise
apps built on top

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:00.780
of Microsoft Fabric, so it's a
complete platform for web apps

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.900
that provides you a backend
with a managed database,

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.260
authentication, hosting,
functions, storage,

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.750
and real-time services.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:13.200
That's great, having all
of this in one place,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.540
but you may already be
using other platforms

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.300
that provide similar features.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.170
What's different here is that it
was built to be enterprise-ready

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.890
and Fabric-native from day one.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:29.310
All the things that you already
like and get from Fabric,

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:32.760
like governance, security,
compliance, and easy integration

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.710
with your Fabric data estates,
you have it with Rayfin.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:40.770
There's also another thing
that I should mention.

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:44.550
It was built from the start
with coding agents in mind,

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:48.330
with the goal of making
vibe-coded apps benefit from all

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:52.960
of these features without any
extra work from your side.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.560
Now that I've set the context,
