00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:59.910
Time for a quick
checkpoint here.

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:04.620
So yes, we just vibe
coded an application,

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:08.220
but the trick is we didn't
ask the agent to figure

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:10.890
out everything on its own.

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:12.540
It didn't have to decide

00:27:12.540 --> 00:27:15.750
from scratch how
authentication should work,

00:27:15.750 --> 00:27:18.210
how the database
should be exposed,

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:21.750
how APIs should be shaped.

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:25.230
Instead, the agent
had guardrails.

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:29.760
It had a workflow designed for
agents, templates to start from,

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:33.030
platforms primitives like
authentication, database,

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:37.380
GraphQL, APIs, and that
makes a big difference.

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:41.940
Because for a recipe sharing
app, things like public

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:45.750
or private recipe, it's
not just UI features.

00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:49.440
They touch identity,
permissions, data access,

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:54.600
and API design, so the value
here is that the agent can focus

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:58.680
on implementing the app
experience while the platform

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:01.260
provides the
foundation around it.

00:28:01.260 --> 00:28:05.970
That's what I mean by vibe
coding with guardrails.

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:09.090
You have fast iteration,
but not from a blank,

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:11.190
unmanaged architecture.

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:14.610
Now comes the important
question, really.

00:28:14.610 --> 00:28:19.660
What does it take to put
this app to production?

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:26.790
What if I say to you that the
app is already in production?

00:28:26.790 --> 00:28:32.250
When we run the npx Rayfin app
