Yeah, this is the result, so I'm
pretty sure the latest version

00:32:08.820 --> 00:32:12.870
of the template is even nicer
because I used an old one,

00:32:12.870 --> 00:32:20.130
but it's already great for just
a few minutes of work to see all

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:23.400
that data coming
from my application.

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:25.740
If there's one more
thing to take away

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:27.720
in this session, it's this.

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:30.090
The fastest path to
production starts

00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:32.100
with the right foundation.

00:32:32.100 --> 00:32:34.890
Coding agents help us move fast.

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:37.890
They are great for bootstrapping
an app, trying ideas,

00:32:37.890 --> 00:32:41.310
and iterating quickly,
but as developers,

00:32:41.310 --> 00:32:44.940
we know that producing code is
only one part of the story.

00:32:44.940 --> 00:32:49.260
A real application still needs
identity, data, security,

00:32:49.260 --> 00:32:53.550
deployments, and a platform
that can support it over time.

00:32:53.550 --> 00:32:57.000
When those pieces are
already in the foundation,

00:32:57.000 --> 00:32:59.580
agents can move quickly
without leaving a lot

00:32:59.580 --> 00:33:02.280
of cleanup for later.

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:05.820
When the app is Fabric
native, the data is already

00:33:05.820 --> 00:33:08.730
where you need it
next for analytics

00:33:08.730 --> 00:33:10.650
and AI-powered insight.

00:33:10.650 --> 00:33:14.400
So yes, by all
means, build fast,

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:16.170
but build on the foundation

00:33:16.170 --> 00:33:20.380
that can take you all
the way to production.

00:33:21.870 --> 00:33:23.910
That's it for this session.

00:33:23.910 --> 00:33:26.760
If you want to learn more
about everything I've talked

